160	  // (Arrow replication — the `prj` channel — is covered by unit tests rather than
161	  // here: driving it end-to-end means creative-mode + inventory juggling + firing
162	  // into open sky to dodge the first-tick despawn, too fragile for a reliable e2e.
163	  // See server/room.test.ts (broadcast) and NetworkSession.test.ts (upsert).)
164	
165	  // ── the friend's own break under latency: local-first, and still propagates ──
166	  // 400±100 ms simulated one-way (~800 ms RTT): the friend digs; the block must
167	  // vanish from the friend's OWN world (its replica commits the break locally,
168	  // ahead of the server), AND the edit must still reach the host over the
169	  // lagged link. That the local commit rides the PREDICTION ledger (a pending
170	  // entry held until the server confirms) is pinned in NetworkSession.test.ts —
171	  // that ledger window is a sub-second internal transient, too narrow to
172	  // observe reliably from a cross-process e2e poll, so here we assert the
173	  // user-visible journey rather than the ledger's internals.
174	  //
175	  // Foreground the friend first: unlike RECEIVING the host's edit (network
176	  // callbacks fire while occluded), the friend's OWN break is engine-rAF-driven,
177	  // and the browser throttles rAF hard on a backgrounded page (the friend has
178	  // sat behind the host all along), so occluded its mining crawls and can miss
179	  // the 45s window. Foregrounded, its engine steps at full rate. (bringToFront
180	  // alone once looked like it broke digging — that was the airborne-aim miss the
181	  // onGround wait in forceDigStraightDown now covers, not bringToFront itself.)
